STYLE: A grand hand-made toy theatre production built from printed card. Every piece is a flat cutout of thick card about 4 mm thick, cut by hand with a craft knife. The front of each piece is printed in the manner of a Victorian wood engraving from an 1840s illustrated newspaper, then tinted by hand with thin transparent watercolour: dense black engraved lines, fine parallel hatching and cross-hatching for shade, soft off-white rag paper, washes that sit loosely over the lines. Figures have the plain, dignified faces of 1840s engravings of Highland crofters. Bold, clear shapes that read from the back row. Puppet joints are brass split pins. Palette: peat brown #5A3E2B, sea grey #6E7C80, slate blue #3B4A5C, heather #7D5A7A, moss green #5E6B3A, bone white #EEE8DA, ink black #1A1817, with ember orange #D9772B used only for fire and glowing peat, and raw card brown #A57E52 at cut edges. The mood is grief, cold, wind, endurance and a family's love.
CONSTRAINTS: No text, no letters, no numbers, no captions, no signature, no watermark, no engraver's initials. No tartan patterns, no clan crests, no flags, no saltire, no crosses, no religious figures, no military. No photographic people, no 3D render look, no plastic, no glossy finish, no anime style, no cartoon style.
REFERENCES: Image 1 is the look reference. Match its Victorian wood engraving line, hatching, thin hand-tinted watercolour, palette, raw card edges and figure style. Do not copy its composition.
SUBJECT: The four pieces of a marionette of Ewan, a young Scottish Highland crofter of twenty-six in the 1840s, facing three-quarters to the right: (1) the head with short dark hair, a soft dark blue bonnet, a steady weathered face with a short beard, and the bare neck; (2) the body from the neck base down to rough leather brogues, in a plain grey homespun belted plaid over a coarse shirt and a dark waistcoat, bare knees, wool hose, standing, with NO arms; (3) his RIGHT arm from shoulder to a strong open hand, shirt sleeve; (4) his LEFT arm from shoulder to a strong open hand, shirt sleeve.
COMPOSITION: A flat lay of the separate cardboard pieces of ONE jointed puppet, all at the same scale, seen straight from the front on a transparent background. Each piece is a separate cutout with clear empty space around it. No piece touches or overlaps another piece. No shadow, no rod, no strings, no stand, no stage.
JOINTS: The body has three clearly visible round brass split pins: one at the centre of the base of the neck and one on each shoulder. The body ends at the neck base with no head and has no arms. The head piece ends in a bare neck with no collar, with one brass pin at the bottom of the neck. Each arm piece has one brass pin at the very top of the shoulder. One arm is a RIGHT arm and the other is a LEFT arm, each hanging straight down from its shoulder pin, with the thumbs on the correct sides. The head is drawn at a natural size for the body.
